      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the ellipse, we start by noting that the ellipse is tangent to the \\(x\\)-axis at \\((4, 0)\\) and tangent to the \\(y\\)-axis at \\((0, 1)\\). This means the center of the ellipse \\((h, k)\\) is at \\((4, 1)\\) because the distance from the center to each tangent line is the length of the semi-major or semi-minor axis.\n\nThe lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes can be determined from the distances of the tangency points. The distance from the center \\((4, 1)\\) to the point \\((4, 0)\\) is the length of the semi-major axis \\(a\\), and the distance from the center \\((4, 1)\\) to the point \\((0, 1)\\) is the length of the semi-minor axis \\(b\\).\n\nThe length of the semi-major axis \\(a\\) is:\n\\[\na = 4 - 4 = 2\n\\]\nThe length of the semi-minor axis \\(b\\) is:\n\\[\nb = 1 - 1 = 1\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci of an ellipse is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\). Substituting the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we get:\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{2^2 - 1^2} = \\sqrt{4 - 1} = \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nThus, the distance between the foci is:\n\\[\n2c = 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nTherefore, the distance between the foci of the ellipse is \\(\\boxed{2\\sqrt{3}}\\).",
