    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the equation of the ellipse, we start by noting that the ellipse is tangent to the \\(x\\)-axis at \\((4, 0)\\) and to the \\(y\\)-axis at \\((0, 1)\\). This means the center of the ellipse is at \\((4, 1)\\), and the lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes are equal to the distances from the center to these tangents. Therefore, the semi-major axis \\(a\\) and the semi-minor axis \\(b\\) are both 4 and 1, respectively.\n\nThe standard form of an ellipse with axes parallel to the coordinate axes is:\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} + \\frac{(y-k)^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\nwhere \\((h, k)\\) is the center of the ellipse, \\(a\\) is the semi-major axis, and \\(b\\) is the semi-minor axis. Substituting the values we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-4)^2}{4^2} + \\frac{(y-1)^2}{1^2} = 1\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-4)^2}{16} + \\frac{(y-1)^2}{1} = 1\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci of an ellipse is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\). Here, \\(a = 4\\) and \\(b = 1\\), so:\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{4^2 - 1^2} = \\sqrt{16 - 1} = \\sqrt{15}\n\\]\nTherefore, the distance between the foci is:\n\\[\n2c = 2\\sqrt{15}\n\\]\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\sqrt{15}}\n\\]",
